          "tagline": "Harbour, Slavery and the City's Own Reckoning",
          "intro": "Bristol got rich on the Atlantic trade and has spent the last thirty years arguing with itself about how to say so. This route runs downhill from the museum at the top of Park Street to the harbour, out to the church Elizabeth I is said to have admired, and back to the market. About 3.4 km. Four of the six stops are free museums or churches you can walk straight into. Note the opening days before you go: some stops on this route close on Mondays and others on Sundays, so a weekday between the two is the safest choice. The walk covers about 3.4 km and takes roughly 44–54 minutes at an easy pace, not counting time spent at each stop.",
          "stops": 6,
