          "sample": "Start on the bridge, because it divides the town and everything else follows from it. The Esk runs out to sea here and the bridge swings open, one half or both, to let boats through to the upper harbour, a bell rings before it moves and the road stops. It is worth waiting for if you catch it starting. One thing in walkers' favour: North Yorkshire Council restricts traffic on the bridge at weekends in the school holidays and on bank holidays, usually between half past ten and four. Special events including the Goth weekends get their own dates and hours, which move from year to year and can run later, so check the council's schedule rather than assuming. It is not fully traffic-free even then: buses are expressly exempt and still cross. But with cars gone it is far easier going. The pavements are narrow otherwise and there is a one-way walking system. Below on both banks the fish quays are working, and the harbour is the reason the town is here at all.",
