          "sample": "Start ten minutes west of the station at a museum that costs nothing to enter. It opened in 1975 as the first national museum outside London, it belongs to the Science Museum Group, and it holds the national collection of rail vehicles: several hundred of them, with around a hundred on display across two enormous halls. General admission is free. Booking a free timed ticket online is recommended at busy periods, but walk-up entry is available every day. Two things to know before you plan around it. Wonderlab, the interactive gallery, is ticketed separately, so 'free' covers the main halls and not everything inside. And the York Central building works have put a temporary detour on the walking approach. It is properly done, the paths along Hudson Boulevard and Foundry Way are signed, lit and separated from traffic, but it is longer than the map suggests, and the rail press has reported visitors walking an extra half mile round the site. Follow the signs rather than your phone, and allow more time than the distance implies in both directions. Most people spend two to three hours here. If you only have an hour, the Great Hall is the one to walk.",
