          "sample": "Start by walking round the outside of the amphitheatre, which costs nothing and takes fifteen minutes. Two tiers of arches run the whole way round, sixty in each tier, and the scale only registers when you follow them and find you are still going. Three square towers rise above the arcades. They are mediaeval, not Roman: after the empire collapsed the arena was turned into a fortified town, with houses and two chapels packed inside the ring and the towers added to defend it, and it stayed that way for centuries until the buildings were cleared out. Inside is eleven euros. The arena is still in use for bullfighting and concerts. It went up in the late first century under Domitian, a hundred and thirty-six metres along its long axis and twenty-one high, with a hundred and twenty arches in all: larger than the arena at Nîmes, and holding over twenty thousand people. Sources disagree on the exact capacity. Four towers were added when it was fortified; three survive.",
