          "sample": "Start at the front that set the tone for the whole city. Victor Louis built the Grand-Théâtre between 1773 and 1780, and it does not look like a theatre: it looks like a temple, which was the point. Twelve Corinthian columns carry an entablature with twelve statues standing on it, one above each column: the nine Muses and three goddesses. Count them from the far side of the square, because from close underneath you cannot see them at all. The interior is blue, white and gold. It opens free from two until six on the first Sunday of each month; guided visits on other dates are ticketed. Come back after dark if you can; the portico is lit and the statues read against the sky. The auditorium was altered during the nineteenth century, but the theatre retains substantial eighteenth-century fabric. A major restoration in 1990 and 1991 reinstated its original blue, white and gold colour scheme.",
