          "sample": "Start with the strangest façade in Colmar. The House of Heads was built in 1609 for a shopkeeper named Anton Burger, and its front carries a hundred and six carved heads, grotesques, faces pulling expressions, animals, crowded across three storeys of stone with no two quite alike. Stand back far enough to take in the whole and then come close and pick them off one by one; the sport is finding the ones that are clearly caricatures of somebody. High on the gable stands a cooper with his barrel, sculpted in 1902 by Auguste Bartholdi, who had designed the Statue of Liberty. The cooper is there for a reason: the Wine Exchange had moved into the building in 1898 and commissioned him. The building is a hotel now, so the street is the visit. The heads are not decoration for its own sake. A merchant house on a main street was an advertisement, and a façade this busy said its owner could afford stone carvers when his neighbours were building in timber.",
