          "sample": "Start at the harbour mouth, where two towers face each other across the water. The name of this one tells you what they were for: a chain was slung between them and winched up at night or in war, closing the port to anything afloat. That is the whole of La Rochelle's history in one object, a town rich enough to be worth attacking and independent enough to expect it. Saint-Nicolas opposite is the taller, and was a fortified residence for the captain and his family with soldiers stationed in it, rather than a bare garrison keep. Standing on the quay between them costs nothing and is the view every photograph of the town is taken from. Saint-Nicolas was begun in the 1330s; the Tour de la Chaîne followed between 1382 and 1390. An explosion in 1651 destroyed this tower's floors and roof. It stood abandoned for roughly three centuries, was reduced in height during the nineteenth, and was restored in major campaigns in the twentieth and twenty-first. A captain lived here until 1472, working the chain and taking the harbour taxes.",
