          "tagline": "Two Quarters and a Long Walk Between Them",
          "intro": "Belfast is in the United Kingdom, so prices here are in pounds rather than euros. The city has two clusters worth walking and a genuine gap between them: the university quarter in the south, with the Ulster Museum and the botanic gardens, and the Victorian centre to the north. This route links them. It is the longest walk in the collection, and one leg is a mostly direct nineteen-minute walk. Walk this route Friday, Saturday or Sunday only: Saint George's Market opens on those three days and is shut the rest of the week, and a closed market hall is a poor reward for a nine-minute detour. Saturday is the most forgiving day. On Friday the market shuts at two, so start around ten and hold the museum to ninety minutes. Allow four to six hours for the whole thing. Every stop is free to experience from outside or within its public areas. Paid interiors are clearly identified, and the free route does not require them. The walk covers about 4.1 km and takes roughly 54–64 minutes at an easy pace, not counting time spent at each stop.",
