          "sample": "Start at the cathedral on the south channel of the Lee. The three spires and the carved stonework are what stop people in the street, and several reviewers say they were simply walking past and had to take photographs. The exterior and the churchyard around it cost nothing and the building is photogenic from almost every angle. The interior charges eight euros for visitors, and this free route stays outside. Tradition traces Cork's beginnings to the early Christian settlement established here by Fin Barre. The present Gothic Revival cathedral was designed by William Burges, consecrated in 1870, and completed when its towers and spires were finished in 1879. The grounds also contain a small outdoor labyrinth designed for quiet reflection. Burges designed the fittings as well as the building, down to the mosaic floor and the sculpture, which is why the interior reads as one piece.",
