          "tagline": "The Walls and What They Look Down On",
          "intro": "Derry is in the United Kingdom, so prices here are in pounds. The city has a complete circuit of seventeenth-century walls around its old centre, and they are free to walk. From the ramparts, you look down into the Bogside, where some of the defining events of the early Troubles took place, including the Battle of the Bogside and Bloody Sunday. This route links the river, the walls, two churches inside them, and the Bogside below. Every stop is free. Start at the Ebrington Square end of the bridge so that you cross the whole span towards the Guildhall. The Peace Bridge is step-free, but the old city is hilly: the walls have a ramped entrance at Magazine Gate, several other entrances use steps, and some surfaces and gradients may be difficult. The quoted walking time covers movement only; with normal stops allow about two and a half to three hours. Both Derry and Londonderry are widely used. Londonderry remains the city's legal name, while Derry appears in the name of the local council and is widely used in everyday speech. The walk covers about 1.9 km and takes roughly 25–35 minutes at an easy pace, not counting time spent at each stop.",
