          "sample": "Start at the Ebrington Square end of the bridge and cross west toward the Guildhall. It opened in 2011, runs two hundred and thirty-five metres across the Foyle, and was built to connect the two sides of the city physically and symbolically: the largely nationalist west bank and the largely unionist Waterside to the east. The curve is deliberate. It is pedestrian and cycle only and step-free from end to end, and the views up and down the river are the reason to walk the whole span rather than just start on it. The two banks were connected by only one bridge for most of the twentieth century, and the Peace Bridge deliberately lands at Ebrington, a former British Army barracks now open as public space. The Foyle is tidal this far up and the water moves visibly under the deck.",
