          "intro": "Dublin's unusual gift to a walker on a budget is that its national institutions charge nothing. The archaeology museum, the national gallery and the Chester Beatty are all free to enter, and so are the grounds of Trinity College and Dublin Castle. This route runs west across the Georgian centre and finishes in the castle grounds. Nine stops, flat and mostly pedestrian, but this is the longest route in the collection: allow a good hour of walking on top of the time spent inside. The one famous thing that does charge is the Book of Kells, and that is covered at the end. The walk covers about 3.7 km and takes roughly 47–57 minutes at an easy pace, not counting time spent at each stop.",
