          "sample": "Start in the Green, the twenty-two acre park at the south end of the centre. It is free, and it is the piece of Dublin most used by the people who live there: lunching office workers, ducks, a bandstand, and a lake with a bridge over it. The square was laid out in the seventeenth century as a common and enclosed for the surrounding householders in the eighteenth; it was opened to the public in 1880 at the expense of Arthur Guinness, whose statue stands in it. The gates close around dusk, which varies a great deal in a city this far north. The Fusiliers' Arch at the north-west corner commemorates the Royal Dublin Fusiliers who died in the Boer War, and was long known locally by a less respectful name. Concerts are held at the bandstand through the summer. The park was landscaped in its present form in the 1870s.",
