          "sample": "Start at the cathedral on the west bank of the Corrib. Entry is free, with donations welcome. The cathedral is usually open from eight in the morning until eight at night, although services and special events may affect access. Completed in 1965 on the site of Galway's former city gaol, it is often described as one of Europe's last great stone cathedrals. The thing reviewers say most often is that it feels far older than it is: the dome, the local marble floor and the stained glass read as centuries old. The organ and the rose window face each other down the length of the nave. Services run through the day and sightseeing gives way to them. The scale of the place is the point: a limestone interior, a Connemara marble floor and a timber barrel-vaulted ceiling beneath the dome.",
