          "sample": "Start away from the crowds at one of Milan's oldest churches. Sant'Ambrogio was founded in the fourth century, when Christianity was still new in the Roman empire, and it is among the oldest churches in Italy. Ambrose was bishop here, and it is his church in a direct sense: he founded it, and he is buried in the crypt beneath the altar, alongside two martyrs whose remains predate him. Whatever you believe, standing in front of that relic puts you about sixteen hundred years back. The building is Romanesque and very plain — no ornament, no colour, nothing to distract you — and it is entered through a wide colonnaded atrium that slows you down before you go in — the architecture is doing something deliberate there. Visitors consistently note how few people are here compared with the Duomo, and how much older it is. Entry is free, with a long midday closure. If you only have time for one interior in Milan, there is a reasonable argument for this one.",
