          "sample": "Start where everyone starts, but stay outside. Walking the full circuit of the Colosseum costs nothing, takes about twenty minutes, and gives you the building's scale better than the interior does. Construction began under Vespasian in AD 72 and it was inaugurated by his son Titus in AD 80, with a hundred days of games; further work continued under Domitian. It is the largest amphitheatre ever built. Two things to notice from outside. The site was not empty ground: Nero had flooded this valley to make an artificial lake for his own palace, and the Flavians drained it and gave the space back to the public as an amphitheatre, which was a deliberate political gesture. And the name is not Roman — the building was the Amphitheatrum Flavium, and Colosseo comes from the colossal statue of Nero that stood beside it, a nickname that only took hold in the Middle Ages. Beside it stands the Arch of Constantine, free and unticketed. Going inside is ticketed and needs booking; the exterior is always open.",
