          "sample": "The commercial heart of the town, triangular, sloping, and paved in herringbone brick. It is named for the cistern at its centre, built in 1287 and capped with an octagonal travertine rim added in 1346 under the mayor Guccio Malavolti, whose coat of arms with its ladder is carved into the stone. The square is ringed by tower-houses and noble palaces, among them the Casa Salvestrini, Palazzo Tortoli and Palazzo Razzi, and the Arco dei Becci. The Torre del Diavolo and the twin Ardinghelli towers rise over it. Of the seventy-two towers that once stood here, fourteen survive. Building them was a contest: the richest families built the tallest they could as a straightforward display of standing. They were also useful. Bridges between towers let families move at height in times of trouble without going down into the street, and the upper floors were good places to handle cloth dyed with saffron, out of the dust and the sun. Saffron was what paid for much of this. Free and always open.",
