          "sample": "Begin at the sanctuary that Turin actually goes to. From the small square it looks quiet and gives almost nothing away: no queues, no signage to speak of. Step inside and the change is abrupt: dense Baroque decoration, painting and sculpture on every surface, in a plan shaped over time by Guarino Guarini, Filippo Juvarra and Carlo Ceppi. Its origins go back much further, to the ninth century. The devotion rests on an event of 20 June 1104. A blind man named Giovanni Ravacchio, who had come from Briançon in France, said the Virgin had told him in a dream where a lost icon was buried; when it was dug out he recovered his sight. No official document records it, but a plaque of 1595 inside reproduces the text of a parchment of 1104. The thing to look for is the wall of ex-voto paintings: hundreds of small hand-painted panels commissioned by ordinary people to record a prayer that was answered, a shipwreck survived, a child recovered, a fall from a cart walked away from. Read a few. They are the most human thing in this city. Entry is free, and the hours are long. One warning: people occasionally try to sell tickets outside. There is no ticket. Across the square is the café where Turin's bicerin, the layered coffee, chocolate and cream drink, has been served for generations.",
