          "sample": "Start where Venetians actually go. The Rialto market has traded on this spot for around a thousand years, and the fish halls beside the Grand Canal are still a working market rather than a display: fishmongers, greengrocers, produce brought in from the lagoon islands. It is one of the very few things in central Venice that costs nothing and is not aimed at visitors. Timing is everything here, and getting it wrong is the most common complaint. There are really two markets with different hours. The fish market runs Tuesday to Saturday, from seven in the morning until about two; the fruit and vegetable stalls run Monday to Saturday and stay open much later. Both are closed on Sunday, and individual stalls often pack up earlier than the official close. By early afternoon the stalls are packing up and what is left is the smell and the rubbish. Come before nine and you will see the thing itself, with the trade boats unloading at the water steps. From here the bridge is two minutes.",
