          "sample": "Start on Piazza Bra at the Roman amphitheatre, and start with the fact that matters: this is not a ruin. It is older than the Colosseum, and it is still a working venue — the opera season plays here every summer, along with concerts, and the 2026 Olympic closing ceremony was held inside it. It has modern lighting, sound, and bathrooms, It was quarried for building stone over the centuries like most Roman monuments, and an earthquake in 1117 brought down almost all of the outer ring — the fragment still standing at one side, the Ala, is what is left of it. What saved the rest was that the city kept finding uses for it and kept repairing it. If you go to a performance, take the stone steps rather than the numbered seats if you want the real thing, and take or hire a cushion — visitors are unanimous that several hours on Roman stone is punishing. Queue well before the start. Walking round the outside and sitting on the square costs nothing; going inside outside performance hours is ticketed and quick.",
