          "sample": "Start at the church the city calls its third cathedral, west of the centre and away from the crowds. The church's original fourteenth-century core is Gothic-Mudéjar, a Christian architectural tradition shaped by Islamic construction and decoration. Its octagonal tower follows the structure of an Almohad minaret, with one tower wrapped around another. Inside are an extraordinary carved altarpiece and a domed chapel at the back. It is rarely crowded. Access is the thing to plan: free visits are offered only on selected dates and times published in a rolling calendar, and are limited to individuals or parties of no more than three. The cloister and the tower require a guided visit. Check the current calendar before you set out, and if no free opening coincides with your visit, treat San Pablo as an exterior stop. Allow twenty minutes; it is the quietest interior on this walk. At sixty-six metres it is Zaragoza's tallest Mudéjar tower and a useful landmark above the western old town.",
